# PART 8 — EDITABLE ARTWORK ENGINE

## 8.1 Initial output format

Use SVG as the primary editable artwork format.

Create real SVG markup with:
- Explicit physical canvas dimensions.
- A correct viewBox.
- Separate text elements.
- Editable vector shapes.
- Logical element groups.
- Stable element IDs.
- Image placements.
- Consistent spacing and alignment.
- Defined colours and typography.
- Optional layer metadata.

Do not generate an SVG that merely contains one flattened image of the whole poster.

## 8.2 SVG architecture

Use meaningful group IDs such as:

- background
- brand-elements
- headline
- hero-visual
- supporting-information
- call-to-action
- disclaimer
- footer

These are examples, not mandatory sections for every artwork.

Store a separate layout specification describing the intended role and position of each major element.

Validate the SVG markup before rendering. Reject malformed output and repair it before export.

## 8.3 Layout specification

Represent the layout in structured data.

For example:

{
  "document": {
    "widthMm": 594,
    "heightMm": 841,
    "orientation": "portrait"
  },
  "elements": [
    {
      "id": "headline",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "primary-message",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 100,
      "width": 470,
      "height": 100,
      "contentSource": "approved-copy.headline",
      "editable": true
    }
  ]
}

This is illustrative. Implement a proper schema and use consistent coordinate units throughout the actual application.

Use a layout engine to translate structured element data into SVG markup. Do not depend on the language model to output an entire complex SVG correctly in a single uncontrolled response.

## 8.4 SVG editor

Create an artwork editor with:
- Live SVG preview.
- Zoom controls.
- Selectable elements where practical.
- Editable text.
- Position and size controls.
- Font and colour controls.
- Layer ordering.
- Asset replacement for non-protected assets.
- Undo and redo.
- Save revision.
- Reset to last saved revision.

Protected assets must remain locked unless the user explicitly changes their permission status.

If full drag-and-drop editing cannot be completed reliably in the first iteration, implement a working property-based editor and keep the source downloadable. Do not display non-functional controls.

## 8.5 SVG security

Treat uploaded SVG files as untrusted content.

Sanitise SVG markup and block scripts, event handlers, external executable references and unsafe embedded content before preview or export.

Do not render arbitrary uploaded markup directly without validation.

