# PART 10 — PDF EXPORT AND PRODUCTION

## 10.1 PDF generation

Generate PDFs from the actual editable SVG source using a reliable SVG-to-PDF rendering library or a suitable server-side rendering pipeline.

Use an appropriate maintained library compatible with the application runtime.

The generated PDF must have the correct physical page dimensions.

For the default A1 poster:
- Trim width: 594 mm.
- Trim height: 841 mm.
- Orientation: portrait.

Use the supplied printer specification to determine bleed, colour profile and other print requirements.

If no printer specification exists, do not invent one. Allow a clearly labelled default preview export and state which settings remain unconfirmed.

## 10.2 Production checks

Check:
- PDF creation success.
- Page dimensions.
- Page count.
- Orientation.
- Font handling.
- Text clipping.
- Image scaling.
- Transparency.
- Vector rendering.
- Asset availability.
- Content integrity.
- Source-to-PDF visual consistency.

Where possible, render the exported PDF back to an image and compare it visually with the approved SVG preview.

Flag any discrepancies.

## 10.3 Font handling

Check whether required fonts are available.

Use embedded fonts where supported and permitted. If fonts cannot be embedded or the renderer does not support a font correctly, report the limitation.

Do not silently substitute fonts that materially alter the layout.

## 10.4 Print limitations

SVG and PDF export alone do not guarantee professional print compliance.

Do not claim that a document uses CMYK, a particular ICC profile, PDF/X compliance, printer-specific bleed or a verified effective image resolution unless the pipeline actually checks or implements those requirements.

Distinguish:
- Editable source generated.
- PDF exported.
- Visual QA passed.
- Print specification verified.
- Human approval received.

These are separate states.

